Answer a failed action with an error instead of a null pointer.

When an action threw inside the wasm, wllama_action logged the reason and returned a null
pointer without writing the length of the response. The worker read that length from the first
four bytes of the request, where the protocol's magic number was still sitting, tried to build a
view of 1163217991 bytes, and the RangeError went through handleError: a signal.abort that
rejects every request the worker was running, not only the one that failed.

The action now answers with the error event that the glue protocol already defines, and the main
thread turns it into a WllamaError for that call alone; the module stays usable. The null pointer
is kept for the case where even that answer cannot be built, and the worker then fails only the
call that caused it.

This is ngxson/wllama#343, applied to the pinned source.

diff --git a/cpp/wllama.cpp b/cpp/wllama.cpp
index b499be1..ca9daf1 100644
--- a/cpp/wllama.cpp
+++ b/cpp/wllama.cpp
@@ -132,7 +132,20 @@ extern "C" const char *wllama_action(const char *name, const char *req_raw)
   catch (std::exception &e)
   {
     printStr(GGML_LOG_LEVEL_ERROR, e.what());
-    return nullptr;
+    // send the error to JS, the caller throws it
+    try
+    {
+      glue_msg_error err;
+      err.message.value = e.what();
+      err.handler.serialize(output_buffer);
+      uint32_t *output_len = (uint32_t *)req_raw;
+      output_len[0] = output_buffer.data.size();
+      return output_buffer.data.data();
+    }
+    catch (std::exception &)
+    {
+      return nullptr;
+    }
   }
 }
 
